From c9d6e4f5a836d255e8cd44ad178856d7076c313e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gaia <build@heygaia.io>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 03:39:40 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(input): pointer input as Chrome dispatches it, scroll
 gestures, and a click that returns before its navigation

CDP mouse input had no pointer events, no mouseMoved handling at all (so no
hover, mouseover or mousemove), a click activation path that duplicated
el.click()'s, and a navigating click that held its command for the whole
page load. Input.synthesizeScrollGesture was "Unknown Input method".

- A press, release and move dispatch pointer events first and the
  compatibility mouse events after them (suppressed from a cancelled
  pointerdown to its pointerup), trusted, with pointerover/out/enter/leave
  and mouseover/out/enter/leave when the pointer reaches another element. A
  press notes user activation; a right press fires contextmenu; a release of
  another button fires auxclick.
- el.click() and a pointer click share one implementation of HTML's click
  activation (checkbox/radio pre-activation, labels, submission, links); a
  link targeting another window opens it through window.open.
- A navigation a command starts (a pointer click on a link, a script's
  form.submit() or location.assign in Runtime.evaluate/callFunctionOn) is
  loaded after the command has answered, through the connection's navigation
  path with its usual events, instead of inline, holding the command (or the
  next one) for the whole load; a single page app route is still reported at
  once. Tests that drove dispatch() directly load the requested navigation
  themselves, as the server does.
- Input.synthesizeScrollGesture is the wheel input it is made of, at its
  point, with its distances (positive towards the start), repeated as asked.
  The wheel's default action walks the flat tree.
- A key press (other than a modifier) notes user activation.

